speechtext:
I have no reason for not doing so. I have read it so I could table it with the committee. It is an affidavit that was made by Rothwell Grant in the city of Montreal in the province of Quebec. The affidavit was sworn before a notary public, commissioner et cetera in the city of Ottawa on the 14th day of April of this year. In the course of this affidavit it is stated that the Minister of Transport met with certain individuals here in Ottawa in the minister's office on the 22nd day of December, 1958 and certain information was given to the minister and certain photographs and photostats relating to the seaway construction together with copies of documents were shown to him and at his request were left with him. The affidavit further states that on two other occasions the same information was supplied to his department. According to the affidavit his department has had and he has himself seen and received the following:
(i)	Minutes of a meeting of seaway general contractors dated February 4th, 1958;
(ii)	Report to St. Lawrence seaway authority contractors on meeting with Mr. F. B. Common, dated February 7th, 1958;

(ill) Notes of a conference of St. Lawrence general contractors, dated February 12th, 1958;
(iv)	Draft outline for brief to Canadian government by a group of contractors on the St. Lawrence seaway;
(v)	Sumary of situation;
(vi)	Warnock-Hersey progress report No. 129.
I do not know of any information other than what is contained in the affidavit except this. If that information is correct-and it is a sworn statement; and I am not blaming the minister because he cannot go around collecting all the documents and everything -if he met with certain people; if he saw certain photographs and documents; and if not less than three copies were in the hands of his department even prior to that time, then I think we are entitled to know by whose authority officials override an order of the House of Commons for a return for all correspondence, telegrams, reports, briefs and other documents exchanged between the Minister of Transport or his deputies and any official or agency of the government, and any person, persons, or organizations since December 1, 1958.
I am not going to say anything more than that. I hope the minister will give an explanation or clarify the situation. Beyond that, I feel there is still a strong interest among the general public and among the members of this House of Commons with regard to what the situation is in connection with the claims of the contractors and the position of the government in respect thereto. I know that several weeks ago certain information was given by the minister. I am hoping that he can now bring us up to date on the situation.
